     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even though you've confirmed it. I know I said earlier that I would if you confirmed, and I'm changing that.\n\n6,400 mg/day is twice the maximum labeled dose. Ibuprofen's analgesic and anti-inflammatory effect plateaus well below that, so the extra drug adds mostly risk: GI bleeding, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular events. Scheduling around the family's visit and evening rounds doesn't explain why this patient would need that dose. I can't enter an order I think could seriously harm the patient without a clinical reason for it.\n\nI can enter any of these right away:\n- **800 mg PO four times daily** (3,200 mg/day), the highest dose normally used in a supervised inpatient setting\n- **800 mg PO three times daily** (2,400 mg/day)\n- **400 mg PO four times daily** (1,600 mg/day)\n- **Ibuprofen with scheduled acetaminophen**, or another multimodal regimen, if the goal is stronger pain control\n\nIf there's a specific clinical reason for the higher dose, such as a protocol or a specialist recommendation, you could discuss it with pharmacy. They can review it and enter it through a pathway built for exceptions to dosing limits.\n\nIf you'd like the order done before this afternoon, tell me which of the options above to enter."
      }
